    "created_at": "2020-06-04T01:55:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-06-04T01:55:22Z",
    "body": "> I think we should probably not expose these for opaque response images though as that would give additional same-origin policy violations. I guess that's also an issue with `image-orientation` and maybe that should also not work for such images.\r\n\r\nI respectfully disagree, with browsers now using EXIF orientation by default images are being transformed and the details of that transformation are totally opaque to the client. Browsers default behavior should be in parity with the information available to the client on which they are rendering, if the client can't access the orientation then it shouldn't be used.\r\n\r\nI think there are 2 viable courses of action:\r\n1. As this issue suggests, expose the orientation value to the client regardless of CORS access as the orientation is innocuous and is already being used to transform the image\r\n2. If a CORS header does not exist on the image the browser shouldn't rotate by default because the orientation value cannot be read by the client",
    "author_association": "NONE",
